# Shell scripts run on Linux build agents — EAS Build, GitHub Actions runners,
# and the pinned Docker image behind the reproducible contract build. On Windows
# `core.autocrlf=true` checks them out with CRLF, and `sh` then reads `set -eu\r`
# and dies with "set: Illegal option -". The EAS CLI archives the *working tree*
# rather than the committed blob, so a clean commit is not enough to prevent it:
# an EAS build failed exactly this way at the post-install hook.
#
# Keeping these LF in the working tree costs Windows editors nothing — every
# editor in use here handles LF — and removes a class of failure that only ever
# appears on a remote machine, minutes into a build.
*.sh text eol=lf
gradlew text eol=lf
*.bash text eol=lf
